    Every integer power answers its base's own type, and a negative power raises where the result is no integer

    Climb batch 20, rung I (wip/rung-integer-power, 97ccd5f1f..981fd6fef), composed from the branch's net change: contract E (P-153). Each integer ^ answers its base's own type; at a negative exponent walk's six power natives give the bases 1 and -1 their exact power, raise DivisionByZero at a base of 0 and IntegerOverflow at any other, and read an exponent of any integer type; QQ's power takes the library's typecase, strToInt writes fail[\ZZ32\], and the ring's and RR64's api powers match their components. The compiled checker refused the library as written ('Function body has type RR64, but declared return type is QQ.' in the rational power, 'ZZ32->ZZ32 is not applicable to an argument of type RR64' in strToFloat): on the flat tower an RR64 holds no integer value, and no type carries a numeral's sign, so no overload can tell 10^16 from 10^(-16). The team tests realArith, compoundArray and simpleExp and the demos BiCGSTAB, BiCGSTAB2 and npbft write a float base, 10.0^(-k).

    Folded from the rung's record: FACTS F-263 added, and F-84's sentence on QQ's power corrected; ledger rows 791 (NEW-I-1) and 792 (NEW-I-2) added, the placeholders numbered in REPORT.md and SKEPTIC.md; the note on row 747 written, its line re-anchored by symbol; the notes on rows 441, 523, 533 and 336 refused for length (the batch's RECORD.md lists them); PLAN, "Climb batch 20, listed for his review", I.worker.1/I.skeptic.1, I.worker.3/I.skeptic.3, I.worker.4/I.skeptic.4, and I.worker.2/I.skeptic.2 in the entry on the team's demos. Rows 438 and 445 close after the rungs.

